{"name":"Donegal Tweed","hex":"#c19964","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tweed","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A coarse woolen fabric used for clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tweed"}],"entries":[{"title":"Donegal tweed","blocks":[{"text":"Donegal tweed is a woven tweed manufactured in County Donegal, Ireland. Originally all handwoven, it is now mostly machine woven and has been since the introduction of mechanised looms in the 1950s-1960s. Donegal has for centuries been producing tweed from local materials in the making of caps, suits and vests. Towards the end of the eighteenth century, The Royal Linen Manufacturers of Ulster distributed approximately six thousand flax spinning wheels and sixty looms for weaving to various Donegal homesteads. These machines helped establish the homespun tweed industry in nineteenth-century Donegal. The term \"Donegal Tweed\" was first publicised by May 1869. Although Donegal tweed has been manufactured for centuries it took on its modern form in the 1880s, largely due to the pioneering work of English philanthropist Alice Rowland Hart."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Donegal_Tweed_label.JPG/330px-Donegal_Tweed_label.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donegal_tweed"},{"title":"Tweed","blocks":[{"text":"Tweed is a rough, woollen fabric, of a soft, open, flexible texture, resembling cheviot or homespun, but more closely woven. It is usually woven with a plain weave, twill or herringbone structure. Colour effects in the yarn may be obtained by mixing dyed wool before it is spun."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Harris_tweed.jpg/330px-Harris_tweed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tweed"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co